{
  "courseSlug": "introduction-to-commodity-trading-gold-silver-crude-and-agri-commodities",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Chapter 2: How Commodity Derivatives Work",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "Commodity Futures Basics: Lot Size, Tick Value, Margin and Expiry",
          "slug": "commodity-futures-basics-lot-size-tick-value-margin-and-expiry",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "c2l1-001",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-001s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A first-time trader sees MCX Gold quoted at ₹1,00,000 and assumes one contract costs ₹1,00,000. It does not. That quote is per 10 grams, and one full Gold contract covers 1 kg, so its value is ₹1 crore. Mistakes like this are how beginners end up with positions 100 times bigger than they intended. By the end of this lesson you will be able to read any commodity futures contract and work out four numbers before you trade: its lot size, its contract value, what every tick is worth, and how much margin it needs, plus when it expires.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l1-002",
              "_type": "bigIdea",
              "text": "Never look at a commodity futures price on its own. Always convert it into rupees per tick and rupees of contract value. Those two numbers, not the quoted price, tell you how much you can make or lose."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l1-003",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-003s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Lot Size and Quotation Unit",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c2l1-004",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-004m1",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The fixed quantity of the commodity covered by one futures contract, such as 1 kg of gold or 100 barrels of crude oil."
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-004m2",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "The quantity the displayed price refers to, such as ₹ per 10 grams for gold or ₹ per barrel for crude oil. It is often different from the lot size."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-004s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Every futures contract has a ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-004s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "lot size",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l1-004m1"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-004s3",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ", the quantity one contract covers, and a ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-004s4",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "quotation unit",
                  "marks": [
                    "c2l1-004m2"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c2l1-004s5",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ", the quantity the displayed price refers to. The two are frequently different, and that gap is where beginners go wrong.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
