              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "scenario",
              "title": "Too big for the account",
              "scenario": "Neha, a 27-year-old product manager in Gurugram, has ₹1,50,000 in her trading account. She is convinced crude oil will rise after an OPEC+ meeting and buys one lot of MCX Crude Oil (100 barrels) at ₹6,500 per barrel, because her broker shows the margin as about ₹1,10,000 and she can afford it. The meeting disappoints and crude falls to ₹6,100 the next day.",
              "prompt": "Work out Neha's loss, explain what it means for her account, and suggest how she should have sized the trade.",
              "modelAnswer": "Loss: (₹6,100 minus ₹6,500) × 100 barrels = minus ₹40,000. That is about 27% of her ₹1,50,000 account and about 36% of the ₹1,10,000 margin, from a price fall of only about 6%.\n\nWhat it means: her account equity drops to ₹1,10,000, leaving essentially no buffer above the margin requirement. Any further fall would force her to add money or have her position squared off by the broker, possibly at the worst price.\n\nBetter sizing: one Crude Oil Mini lot (10 barrels) would have had a tick value of ₹10 instead of ₹100, and the same move would have cost ₹4,000, under 3% of her account. A sensible approach is to decide the maximum rupee loss she is willing to accept on a trade first, say 1% to 2% of capital, and pick the contract size and exit level that fit within it."
